---
title: 'Stock Options and ESOPs Explained: How to Value Startup Equity Before You Accept an Offer'
publishedAt: 2026-11-11
type: job-seekers
---
**Quick answer:** Stock options (called ESOPs in India) give you the right to buy company shares later at a fixed price, the strike or exercise price. They usually vest over four years with a one-year cliff, which means nothing vests until your first anniversary. To value an offer, find out how many options you get, the strike price, the latest valuation, the total number of shares outstanding, and the exercise window if you leave. Treat early-stage equity as a lottery ticket with good odds at best, and never accept a lower salary than you can live on because of it.

Startup offers often include equity, and it can be worth a lot or nothing. Most candidates don't know what questions to ask, so they either ignore equity or overvalue it. This guide explains the terms and gives you a simple way to compare offers.

## What are stock options?

A stock option is the right to buy a share of the company at a fixed price in the future. That price is set when the options are granted, usually based on a recent valuation.

If the company grows and its shares become worth more than your strike price, the difference is your gain. If the company fails or is sold for less than its preferred investors put in, your options may be worth nothing.

## What is an ESOP?

ESOP stands for Employee Stock Option Plan. In India, "ESOPs" is the common word for the options themselves. In the US, startups more often say "stock options" and the plan is an "equity incentive plan".

