## Can you negotiate equity?

Yes. Equity is often easier to move than salary at an early startup, because it doesn't cost cash today. You can ask for:

- More options.
- A longer exercise window.
- Single- or double-trigger acceleration on acquisition.
- A one-time refresh after a year.

See our [offer negotiation guide](https://gethired.devhub.co.in/blog/how-to-negotiate-a-tech-job-offer-in-2026-salary-equity-and-scripts-that-work) for scripts.

## Frequently asked questions

### Are startup stock options worth it?

They can be, but most of the value comes from a small number of very successful companies. Accept an offer because the cash, role and team are right, and treat equity as upside.

### What happens to my options if I leave?

Unvested options are usually cancelled. Vested options must normally be exercised within the exercise window, or they expire. Check your plan document.

### What happens to my options if the company is acquired?

It depends on the deal and your plan. Options may be converted into the buyer's equity, paid out in cash, accelerated, or cancelled if the sale price is below what investors are owed.

### What is a good number of options for an engineer?

There's no standard number, because share counts differ by company. Compare percentages of fully diluted shares instead, and compare them against offers at a similar stage.

### Do I have to exercise my options?

No. Exercising costs money and may trigger tax, so many employees wait until there's a sale or a buyback. The risk is that options can expire if you leave and don't exercise in time.

